meta {
  name: Get Performance (Invalid Custom)
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/performance?range=custom
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:query {
  range: custom
}

tests {
  test("rejects a custom range with no bounds", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(400);
  });

  test("says which field is wrong", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().message).to.equal("Validation failed");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /performance?range=custom (no bounds) — `400`

  A negative test kept in the collection because this is the failure the web
  client is built to avoid rather than to handle.

  `range=custom` without `from`/`to` is reachable by ordinary means — a shared
  link truncated at `?range=custom`, or a reload while the picker is open — so
  the **client degrades to the default range** instead of sending this request.
  Verified in the browser: such a URL renders month-to-date figures, not an
  error state.

  The API still rejects it, because a server should not invent a window the
  caller did not ask for.

  ## Also `400`
  | query | why |
  |-------|-----|
  | `from=2026-02-01&to=2026-01-01` | `from` after `to` |
  | `from=2026-02-31&to=2026-03-01` | February 31st does not exist |
  | `from=2020-01-01&to=2026-01-01` | span over the 366-day cap |
  | `range=fortnight` | not in the enum |

  ## Response `400`
  ```json
  {
    "message": "Validation failed",
    "errors": {
      "formErrors": [],
      "fieldErrors": { "from": ["A custom range needs both from and to."] }
    }
  }
  ```
}
